# Benchmarks

Measured with `iperf3` (throughput) and `ping` (latency) over isolated Linux network namespaces connected via `veth` pairs on the same host. This eliminates external network interference and measures tunnel overhead in isolation. Real-world numbers will be lower and vary with RTT, packet loss, and CPU.

Results are from v0.1.2 on Linux x86_64.

## Throughput

### UDP Transport (QUIC)

| Parallel streams | Throughput |
|-----------------|------------|
| 1 | 2.4 Gbps |
| 2 | 2.4 Gbps |
| 3 | 2.4 Gbps |
| 4 | 2.4 Gbps |
| 5 | 2.4 Gbps |

### TCP Transport (WebSockets)

| Parallel streams | Throughput |
|-----------------|------------|
| 1 | 2.4 Gbps |
| 2 | 2.1 Gbps |
| 3 | 2.3 Gbps |
| 4 | 2.5 Gbps |

Both transports achieve equivalent throughput on low-loss networks.

### Reverse Tunnel (QUIC)

Reverse tunnels are limited by single-stream QUIC performance in the connecting direction.

| Parallel streams | Throughput |
|-----------------|------------|
| 1 | 581 Mbps |

## Latency

Round-trip latency measured with ICMP ping through the full tunnel stack.

| Transport | Min | Avg | Max | Jitter |
|-----------|-----|-----|-----|--------|
| UDP (QUIC) | 0.083 ms | 0.164 ms | 0.245 ms | 0.034 ms |
| TCP (WebSockets) | 0.055 ms | 0.129 ms | 0.202 ms | 0.034 ms |

WebSocket offers ~21% lower average RTT on low-loss networks due to kernel-space TCP optimization. On high-loss or high-jitter links, QUIC's per-stream loss recovery prevents head-of-line blocking.

Zero packet loss was observed for both transports at all tested rates.
